# Platform / test-gated Rust symbols

Rustc only sees the current target. A symbol used solely under `#[cfg(target_os = "...")]` or in tests looks unused on other builds and triggers `unused_imports` / `dead_code`.

## Prefer cfg over allow

Gate the definition or re-export so it is not compiled where unused. Do not reach for `#[allow(dead_code)]` / `#[allow(unused_imports)]` when a cfg matches the real callers.

```rust
// ✅ GOOD — re-export only where callers compile
pub use pipeline::transcode_file_with_metadata;

#[cfg(target_os = "windows")]
pub use pipeline::encode_pcm_with_metadata;
#[cfg(target_os = "windows")]
pub use pcm_stream::PcmStream;

// ✅ GOOD — test-only helper
#[cfg(test)]
pub fn write_pcm_to_wav(...) { ... }

// ❌ BAD — still compiled (and warned) on macOS app builds
#[cfg(any(test, target_os = "macos"))]
pub fn write_pcm_to_wav(...) { ... }

// ❌ BAD — hides real dead code
#[allow(dead_code)]
pub fn write_pcm_to_wav(...) { ... }
```

## Checklist when adding cross-platform APIs

1. Identify which OS / `cfg(test)` paths call the symbol.
2. Put the same cfg on `pub use`, `pub fn`, or the module that owns it.
3. Keep shared helpers used on every target ungated.
4. After changing cfgs, confirm a local `tauri`/`cargo` build is warning-clean for unused items.
